    "created_at": "2017-07-12T17:53:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-07-12T17:53:11Z",
    "body": "> How can we make the recursion even less worse? I know in [whatwg/console](https://github.com/whatwg/console) we use abstract algorithms that can actually be called with parameters and yield results. I feel like this algorithm could benefit from pulling the nested `<ol>` out into its own algorithm. Is this a common thing in most standards?\r\n\r\nYep, this is quite common. In HTML we define the algorithms a little less formally, using `<dfn>`s and no headings. An example would be, e.g. https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#window-open-steps\r\n\r\n> Substep 2 checks to see `If node has no associated CSS box`...could we make this `If node is not being rendered` and still be ok?\r\n\r\nI think we should. I think the use of associated CSS box was an oversight from forgetting that \"being rendered\" existed.",
