    "body": "> When is it correct to use a `<span>` vs `<code data-x>` vs `<a>`?\r\n\r\nHTML uses Wattsi instead of Bikeshed, which is unfortunately not documented very well. Maybe we should do that. Here are some rough translations:\r\n\r\n- `<a>` <-> `<span>`\r\n- `<a lt=\"...\">` <-> `<span data-x=\"...\">`\r\n- `{{x}}` <-> `<code data-x=\"...\">`\r\n\r\nYou almost never use `<a>` in HTML (only for referencing sections, generally).\r\n\r\n> What exactly does the `data-x` attribute represent (on `code` for example) and why do we use it?\r\n\r\nIt corresponds to the `data-x` of the definition you are linking to. (Or the implied `data-x`; if you do e.g. `<dfn>foobar</dfn>`, then the implied `data-x` is `foobar`. This then is useful when doing `<span data-x=\"foobar\">foobars</span>`.)\r\n\r\n`data-x` values are also used to generate IDs.\r\n\r\n> What is the purpose of `<div w-nodev>`? As in, what does this attribute signify and how many things should go inside of this kind of div?\r\n\r\nAh! This is important, and we did manage to document it. https://github.com/whatwg/html/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#the-developers-edition\r\n\r\nEverything algorithmic should be inside a w-nodev container.",
